 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_TECH_TRADE_PEDIA</Tag>
-		<English>[TAB]When tribes met they often traded goods like furs to pottery, fish to deer meat etc. Trading goods eventually led to written language and mutually used currencies.</English>
-		<French>[TAB]When tribes met they often traded goods like furs to pottery, fish to deer meat etc. Trading goods eventually led to written language and mutually used currencies.</French>
-		<German>[TAB]When tribes met they often traded goods like furs to pottery, fish to deer meat etc. Trading goods eventually led to written language and mutually used currencies.</German>
-		<Italian>[TAB]When tribes met they often traded goods like furs to pottery, fish to deer meat etc. Trading goods eventually led to written language and mutually used currencies.</Italian>
-		<Spanish>[TAB]When tribes met they often traded goods like furs to pottery, fish to deer meat etc. Trading goods eventually led to written language and mutually used currencies.</Spanish>
+		<English>[TAB]At a river crossing or a seasonal gathering, goods could travel farther than the people who made them. Salt, grain, pottery and metal passed between communities with different resources and skills. Exchange took many forms, including gifts, credit, barter and, in some societies, money; these did not follow one universal sequence. As trade networks grew, merchants depended on trusted measures, protected routes and reliable partners. Along those routes traveled techniques, stories and beliefs as well as cargo.</English>
+		<French>[TAB]À un gué ou lors d'un rassemblement saisonnier, les marchandises pouvaient voyager plus loin que leurs fabricants. Sel, céréales, poteries et métaux circulaient entre des communautés aux ressources et aux savoir-faire différents. Les échanges prenaient diverses formes : dons, crédit, troc et, dans certaines sociétés, monnaie, sans suivre partout la même succession. Avec l'essor des réseaux commerciaux, les marchands avaient besoin de mesures fiables, de routes protégées et de partenaires dignes de confiance. Techniques, récits et croyances accompagnaient les cargaisons.</French>
+		<German>[TAB]An einer Flussfurt oder bei einem jahreszeitlichen Treffen konnten Waren weiter reisen als die Menschen, die sie herstellten. Salz, Getreide, Keramik und Metall wechselten zwischen Gemeinschaften mit unterschiedlichen Rohstoffen und Fertigkeiten. Der Austausch kannte viele Formen: Geschenke, Kredit, Tauschhandel und in manchen Gesellschaften Geld. Eine allgemeingültige Abfolge gab es nicht. Mit wachsenden Handelsnetzen wurden verlässliche Maße, geschützte Wege und vertrauenswürdige Partner wichtiger. Neben Waren verbreiteten sich auch Techniken, Geschichten und Glaubensvorstellungen.</German>
+		<Italian>[TAB]Presso un guado o durante un raduno stagionale, le merci potevano viaggiare più lontano di chi le produceva. Sale, cereali, ceramiche e metalli passavano tra comunità dotate di risorse e competenze diverse. Gli scambi assumevano molte forme: doni, credito, baratto e, in alcune società, denaro, senza seguire una sequenza universale. Con la crescita delle reti commerciali, i mercanti avevano bisogno di misure affidabili, percorsi protetti e soci fidati. Lungo quelle vie viaggiavano tecniche, racconti e credenze, oltre alle merci.</Italian>
+		<Spanish>[TAB]En un vado o durante una reunión estacional, las mercancías podían viajar más lejos que quienes las producían. La sal, los cereales, la cerámica y los metales circulaban entre comunidades con distintos recursos y conocimientos. El intercambio adoptaba muchas formas: regalos, crédito, trueque y, en algunas sociedades, dinero, sin seguir una secuencia universal. Al crecer las redes comerciales, los mercaderes necesitaban medidas fiables, rutas protegidas y socios de confianza. Por esos caminos viajaban técnicas, relatos y creencias, además de mercancías.</Spanish>
 		<Finnish></Finnish>
 		<Hungarian></Hungarian>
 		<Polish></Polish>
